- z| = \\sqrt{(Re(x) - Re(z))^2 + (Im(x) - Im(z))^2}$, where $Re(x)$ and $Im(x)$ are the real and imaginary parts of $x,$ respectively.\n\nNow, let's use the fact that $x + y + z = 0$:\n\n$$ x - z = -x - y + 2z $$\n$$ |x - z| = |-x - y + 2z| $$\n\nWe know that $x + y + z = 0,$ so we can rearrange this equation to get $z = -x - y.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$ |x - z| = |-x - y - (-x - y)| $$\n$$ |x - z| = |-x - y + x + y| $$\n$$ |x - z| = |0| $$\n$$ |x - z| = 0 $$\n\nSince the base of the isosceles triangle is zero, we get a contradiction. This means that the base $b$ of the isosceles triangle cannot be zero. Therefore, the base $b$ must be two times the magnitude of the imaginary part of $x - z.$\n\nTo find the magnitude of $x - z,$ we can use the formula mentioned earlier:\n\n$$ |x - z| = \\sqrt{(-Re(z))^2 + (Im(x) - Im(z))^2} $$\n\nSince we don't know the exact values of $Re(z)$ and $Im(x) - Im(z),$ we can't find the exact value of $b.$ However, we can express the base $b$ in terms of the given information:\n\n$$ b = 2|Im(x - z)| $$\n\nTherefore, the base $b$ of the isosceles triangle formed by the points $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ is $2|Im(x - z)|.$\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{7648}$ because the base $b$ is the distance between two of the points, which can be calculated using the given information.",
